---
title: Hex reverse
tags: tools
heading: "Reverse hex"
---
{% from "macros/tool.njk" import toolSection with context %}
{% from "macros/tool-frame.njk" import toolFrame %}
{% from "macros/delimiter.njk" import delimiter %}
{% set filename = page.fileSlug %}
{% set intro %}
<p class="px-2">
    Unlike reversing the entire string where <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> [Hello] becomes <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">f6 c6 c6 56 84</code>, here we have some unique ways to reverse hex strings.</p>
    <dl class="row g-0 px-2">
        <dt>Reverse bytes</dt>
        <dd>This reverses the order of bits within each byte. <code
            class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> becomes <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">84 56 c6 c6 f6</code></dd>
        <dt>Reverse byte order</dt>
        <dd>While it sounds similar, byte order is a little different. This reverses the order of each hex byte in the string, so <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> becomes <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">6f 6c 6c 65 48</code></dd>
        <dt>Reverse nibbles</dt>
        <dd>This reverses the bits within each nibble (4 bits) of every byte in a hexadecimal string, which is equivalent to: converting the hex string to binary, reversing the bits in each nibble, then converting it back to hexadecimal. <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">48 65 6c 6c 6f</code> becomes <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">21 6A 36 36 F6</code></dd>
    </dl>
</p>
<p class="px-2">
    Your hex can use any of the usual delimiters between bytes (spaces, hyphens, colons, commas, semicolons, new lines, \x, 0x or %), or none, and they can be mixed. The delimiter dropdown chooses how the result is written.
</p>
{% endset %}
{% call toolSection(intro) %}
{% call toolFrame(filename, "Reverse hex", placeholder = "Example: 48 65 6c 6c 6f") %}
<div class="input-group w-auto">
    <label class="input-group-text" for="{{ filename }}Type"><span class="visually-hidden">What to </span>Reverse</label>
    <select class="form-select" id="{{ filename }}Type" autocomplete="off">
        <option value="bytes" selected="">Bytes</option>
        <option value="byte-order">Byte order</option>
        <option value="nibbles">Nibbles</option>
    </select>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-convrtr" id="{{ filename }}Run">Reverse</button>
</div>
<div class="input-group w-auto">
    <label class="input-group-text" for="{{ filename }}Delimiter"><span class="visually-hidden">Hex </span>Delimiter</label>
    {{ delimiter(filename) }}
</div>
{% endcall %}
{% endcall %}
